From 6bf1a7358ff097028d6058f7d11fad72270f6b82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 16:15:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 112/118] tls: fingerprint fallback, what two reviews and the
 stands showed

The fallback is turned on and off, and given its list, by the option
experimental.utls_fallback of the configuration as well, so a front end
can decide per configuration. With the option SING_BOX_UTLS_FALLBACK is
not read; a list that names nothing is the default one; and the option is
registered for every configuration, also one without it, so that a
configuration loaded again (SIGHUP) does not keep that of the one before
where the instances of a process share the registry (1.13).

The default list is safari, randomized, edge, firefox, chrome. First the
two whose ClientHello carries no ECH extension; then edge, whose
ClientHello is small (517 bytes, no X25519MLKEM768 key share), for a path
that drops the large ones. On the stand, on a path to a TLS server that
drops every ClientHello over 1000 bytes, the first request fails after
2.6 s and four handshakes and the next ones pass with edge; with the list
without edge none of six passes.

REALITY. A REALITY client goes through the same list, without android
and 360 only (a ClientHello of TLS 1.2, which REALITY cannot work with).
The fingerprints without the X25519MLKEM768 key share (edge, ios, qq) are
tried as the rest: a server older than Xray-core v26.9.8 takes them, and
there are many of those. A newer one answers such a ClientHello as a
stranger's, with the certificate of its target. That failure names the
fingerprint and is told from the others by its type, and the fallback
goes on with the next fingerprint, also when the one refused has worked
on the path: that is a server updated meanwhile, and the path would
otherwise stay on a fingerprint the server never takes again. Any other
failure of a fingerprint that has worked, and any failure of the
configured one, is the server's business as before. On the stand (every
ClientHello over 1000 bytes dropped): with Xray 26.6.27 the first request
fails after 2.6 s and the next ones pass with edge; with 26.9.9 edge is
refused in 0.05 s and none of six passes, as nothing can; with the
server updated from the one to the other while edge is in use, edge is
left at the first refusal, and where the path drops nothing from then
on, the same dial goes on with firefox and 29 requests of 30 pass (the
one that does not is the first of the run).

When to leave a fingerprint. The configured one, and one that has
completed a handshake on the path, is left after two unanswered
ClientHellos in a row, the second on a connection made after the first
was given up: one unanswered ClientHello may be a second of loss. A
candidate of the list that has not worked yet is left after one, and
also when the server answers it and refuses the handshake (ECH it cannot
carry, a version of TLS the server does not speak): it would otherwise
stay in use for good. A handshake that waited out its whole timeout
moves the path on and ends the dial. A dial makes four handshakes at
most.

When the cause does not choose by fingerprint, the fallback rests, and
connections are made exactly as without it:
- the whole list went unanswered (the address or the name is blocked,
  the server is down): 30 s, twice as long when the next such rest
  begins soon after the last one, up to 10 min. The path rests on the
  fingerprint that worked there last, not on the configured one: where
  the configured one is dropped, a blackout of a few seconds would cost
  the whole rest of connections made with a ClientHello known not to
  pass;
- the fingerprint that went unanswered is answered on the next
  connection, a loss: 30 s every time. After three such rests in a row
  the next unanswered ClientHello moves the path on at once: what drops
  a part of the ClientHellos of one fingerprint looks like a loss each
  time. The rests are in a row when the ClientHello that began each was
  among the first ten sent since the rest before it; five quiet minutes
  begin the count anew.

What the fallback says is written at the warning level, the same line
once in ten seconds with the number of repeats; the verdict of a list
tells how many candidates went unanswered and how many were refused.

The watch of a ClientHello ends on the first byte from the server, not
on the first acknowledgment: with tls.fragment the first piece is
acknowledged long before the ClientHello is.

Detours. Inside the connection of another outbound the socket is not the
server's: the fallback is not used there, and tls.spoof is refused with
an error ("tls.spoof does not work through a detour to a proxy
outbound"), since the forged ClientHello would be sent to the server of
the tunnel, into its connection. A detour to a direct outbound (another
uplink, by its interface or mark) is a socket to the server as any
other: both work. It is decided at the dial, by what the detour leads
to; a detour that leads nowhere says so itself. The wrappers of a client
configuration (ECH taken from DNS, kernel TLS) answer for tls.spoof of
what they wrap; the fallback itself does not reach a wrapped
configuration.
